

                                 jamerror

================================================================================

C++ error handling strategy for high quality user feedback and intentional
public APIs.


Goals
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- Make it obvious that a function may error.
- Have errors represented in a standardised way so checking for them is the same
  every time.
- Control the depth of error that is visible to the public API user.


How to Use
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Include the header
#include "jamerror.hpp"

// Define your functions with a jamerror reference argument.
// It's imperitive that it's a reference or at least a pointer so the original
// instance gets modified.
void function_that_can_fail(jamerror& err) {

  // If an error occurs in the function, append a message to the error.
  if (/* your error condition */) {
    err.append("Error running function that can fail.");
    return;
  }
}

int main() {
  // Declare your jamerror.
  jamerror err;
  // Each thread should declare it's own jamerror. Sharing between threads could
  // result in errors from one thread being visible in another.

  // Call your function that can fail and pass the error reference.
  function_that_can_fail(err);
  // Check to see if the function failed.
  if (err) {
    // Add more information at this higher level if you want to.
    err.append("Error running program!");

    // Print the error message log to stderr.
    // This also returns the error status which is set to 1 by append by default
    // so can be used as the exit code for the program too.
    return err.print();
  }

  // Assuming you always exit on failure or clear a non-fatal error with
  // err.clear(), you can re-use the jamerror.
  function_that_can_fail(err);
  if (err) {
    return err.append("Error running program!").print();
  }
}

// You can also define your own error statuses to allow branching on different
// errors in a way that gives you control over the public API of your function.
// Start your custom error status ints at 2 because 0 and 1 are used for
// "jamerror::status_ok" and "jamerror::status_internal_error" already.

// You probably wouldn't want to do this after your main function like this but
// this is just an example.

static constexpr my_custom_error_status = 2; // Or use an enum

void another_function_that_can_fail(jamerror& err) {
  if (/* your error condition */) {
    err.append("Error running function that can fail.", my_custom_error_status);
    return;
  }
}


Reference
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

See the jamerror header at "include/jamerror.hpp", it has a lot of explanatory
comments.


GNU Make Commands
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Build: "make", archive is generated in "./bin/".

Test: "make test"

Install: "make install"

Uninstall: "make uninstall"


Alternatives
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Exceptions

- Nice for implicit error handling thanks to how it "bubbles" errors up to
  the nearest catch statement.
- Providing additional context for an error requires a try catch block at
  every level which can be a little more cumbersome that say, returning
  an error and checking it.
- Makes it unobvious if a function can throw an exception unless it's defined as
  "noexcept". Should we try-catch every function that's not noexcept?
- Can expose implementation specific exceptions which API users can make
  decisions based on, therefore making your public API dependent on your
  implementation. Better have a tight specification!.

std::expected

- Similar to the loved and hated returning of errors in Go.
- Good at explicitly showing that a function can error.
- Can be a little clunky to frequently use "std::expected<T, E>",
  "expected.has_value()", "expected.error()", etc.
- std::expected get instantiated on every function call that uses it even when
  there is no error.

Invalid returns

- Returning NULL, -1 or who knows what? go read the docs again.
- Very compact as it uses the existing return value to communicate that
  something went wrong.
- Often requires more detail, i.e. provided by "errno" which is not always set
  must be reset first for some functions but not most.
- Good for high performance but easy to make mistakes without scouring
  documentation frequently.
